    @section pgsqlsandbox Sandboxing

    In a %Qore version that checks DBI connections against the sandbox, a connection opened by code governed by a
    sandbox is checked against its network and filesystem policy before it is opened.  The driver reports every
    destination that libpq would connect to, completing the connection parameters with libpq's defaults as libpq does
    (the \c PGHOST, \c PGHOSTADDR and \c PGPORT environment variables and the compiled-in port and socket directory):
    - every entry of a multi-host list (\c "host1,host2"), with its port
    - a host by TCP, or the address given with \c PGHOSTADDR instead of the host name
    - a host that is a directory, or no host, as the UNIX socket <tt>.s.PGSQL.&lt;port&gt;</tt> in that directory or in libpq's
      default socket directory, which the filesystem policy decides

    A connection that uses a service (\c PGSERVICE) is refused with \c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED, as the service file can
    name any destination, and so is an abstract UNIX socket (a host starting with \c "@"), which no filesystem policy
    governs.

    A connection that was checked connects to the addresses that were checked only: each address becomes an entry of
    the connection with the address as libpq's \c hostaddr and the host name as \c host, so that a host name that
    resolves to another address later (also when the driver reconnects or skips a host that timed out) cannot
    redirect it, while TLS host name verification, \c .pgpass and GSSAPI still use the host name.  A host name with
    several addresses therefore has more weight with \c load_balance_hosts=random.

    Pinning the connection needs libpq 12 or later; with an older libpq, code governed by a sandbox that restricts
    the network or the filesystem cannot open a connection.

    @section pgsqltrans Transaction Management

    When the Datasource is not in auto-commit mode, this driver automatically inserts \c "begin" statements at the start of each transaction to provide consistent transaction handling across all Qore DBI drivers; it's not necessary to do this manually.

    Note that an exception while already in a transaction will have the effect that the pgsql connection cannot be used until the transaction is closed; this is different from most other current Qore DBI drivers. An exception in a Datasource::exec() method as the first statement of a transaction will cause an automatic implicit \c "rollback" to be executed to maintain compatibility with other Qore DBI drivers.

